<title>SOUTH AFRICA: GOLD FIELDS GHANA HALF YR NET INCOME DROPS.</title>
<headline>GOLD FIELDS GHANA HALF YR NET INCOME DROPS.</headline>
<dateline>JOHANNESBURG 1997-01-15</dateline>
<text>
<p>Half year to end Dec, 1996</p>
<p>			(millions of $US unless stated)</p>
<p>    Tonnes milled		 136,690 vs 142,820</p>
<p>    Gold output (ounces)	 22,979 vs  23,067</p>
<p>    Average price ($US)	     381 vs  385</p>
<p>    Cost and expenses	     8.38  vs  7.65</p>
<p>    Sales and other income	9.01  vs 8.89</p>
<p>    Costs and expenses	    8.38  vs 7.65</p>
<p>    Pre-tax income		  0.63  vs 1.23</p>
<p>    Tax				 0.28  vs 0.45</p>
<p>    Net income			0.35  vs 0.78 </p>
<p>Full name: Golds Fields Ghana Ltd</p>
<p>NOTE - The firm said the feasibility study for the</p>
<p>authorised starter project for its new surface mine had been</p>
<p>completed and the project approved. Project implementation had</p>
<p>now started and orders placed for most major items needed for</p>
<p>construction.</p>
<p>The firm added the first gold pour is seen in a year's time</p>
<p>with production scheduled to build up from 100,000 ounces of</p>
<p>gold in 1998 to about 250,000 ounces by 2000 at a capital cost</p>
<p>of $125 million.</p>
<p>The firm also plans the construction of facilities to treat</p>
<p>the less weathered material at the site which should eventually</p>
<p>double production from the 13 million ounce resource to 500,000</p>
<p>ounces a year. The mine's life is seen at in excess of 20 years.</p>
<p>-- Johannesburg newsroom +27 11 482 1003</p>
</text>
